Compact EEG decoders represent each window by the log band power of learned
spatial filters, which capture the covariance between channels only implicitly.
We add a tangent-space branch that maps each window's shrinkage-regularised
covariance to the tangent space at a label-free running reference updated during
inference. Across five cohorts (51 participants, three data-split seeds), the
branch improved balanced accuracy in four cohorts: by
$0.064$ on a 20-participant motor-execution cohort held out from development
($p=8.8\times10^{-5}$, 20 of 20 participants improving), $0.040$ on BCI
Competition IV-2a ($p=0.020$), $0.021$ on a second exoskeleton cohort
($p=0.047$) and $0.045$ on a longitudinal exoskeleton cohort ($p=0.219$); 40 of
the 43 participants improved. On these cohorts it achieved the highest mean
balanced accuracy among eight published decoders trained in the same pipeline
without per-model tuning. A classical tangent-space classifier scored $0.116$
lower on the longitudinal cohort and similarly on the held-out cohort. On the
fifth cohort the branch reduced balanced accuracy by $0.175$. Its median class
block lasts 214 windows, longer than the reference's nominal memory of 160,
whereas the other cohorts have median blocks of 1 to 18 windows. Varying the
estimator memory across the block length changed the direction and size of the
effect ($+0.045$ at 160 windows, $+0.048$ at 40, $-0.076$ at 8). A pre-specified
rule for setting the adaptation rate from the block length was not supported.
Adaptive tangent-space features can improve cross-session EEG decoding, but
their effect depends on the temporal structure of the test stream relative to
the reference memory.
